00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:04.860
TOM NIKL: Hi.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:05.672
I'm Tom Nikl.

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.380
And today, I'm going
to talk a little bit

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.330
about cloud migration.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.490
Maybe you've already decided
to take your business

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.560
into the cloud, and
you're evaluating

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.950
the right combination of
tools to make that happen.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.600
Or maybe your heart isn't
set on migrating just yet,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.680
and you're analyzing
the benefits.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.870
Either way, I'm
happy you're here.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.540
I don't get to do stuff
like this very often.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.060
Now, there's no single
reason that businesses

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.010
migrate but a few that
come up quite a bit.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.920
Tell me if any of
these sound familiar--

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.940
increased capacity requirements,
software or hardware refresh

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.870
cycles, data center
contract renewals coming up.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.580
The list goes on, and you
should check out our blog post

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.880
to see if any of these
examples resonate personally.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.230
I've got time.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.070
OK.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.260
Of course, every company's
story is a little different.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.560
And so deciding if
you're ready to migrate

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.750
is really the same
as asking, how will I

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.940
use the cloud once I'm there?

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.310
Or better still,
which combination

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:08.540
of Google Cloud's storage,
compute, networking, security,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.920
databases, and more can get
me to the most agile, secure,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.840
functionally integrated
cloud architecture?

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.610
Phew.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.620
That was a mouthful, right?

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.470
This can be a big but
simultaneously very

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.480
fun question to answer.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.070
That's why, for starters, a
detailed cost-benefit analysis

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.520
is so important.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.100
If you're not sure
where to start,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.780
Google's trusted
partners can take

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.630
stock of your unique
operational landscape

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.360
with a realistic return on
investment for every choice

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.710
baked right in.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.630
That way, you'll
know exactly how

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.450
you stand to reduce things
like operational overhead

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.880
and unused compute
if you work to make

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.550
the switch to the cloud,
all before you ever

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.380
commit to a migration plan.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.070
Next, it's important to remember
that your journey to the cloud

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.260
is your journey to the cloud.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.110
You'll decide if you want
to move stuff as is first

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:04.070
and keep it there, or maybe you
modernize it after you move it.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.830
Or maybe you want to
modernize first, then migrate.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.500
Maybe you just want to rebuild
a few things altogether.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.810
So where do you begin?

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.390
For the workloads that you're
looking to lift and shift,

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.420
you'll turn to Migrate for
Compute Engine, a tool that

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.230
was purpose-built to move
physical servers or VMs

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.880
into Google Compute Engine.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.780
It's fast and scalable and
has cool stuff like testing,

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.620
right sizing, and stateful
rollback built right in.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.820
Want more info?

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.210
Here's a website you
may want to check out.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.970
What about workloads you
want to modernize as well?

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.640
For that, you'd use
Migrate for Anthos,

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.830
which lets you upgrade
workloads directly

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.590
into containers in GKE.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.890
That means you can take
physical servers or VMs that are

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.400
on-prem, in GCE,
or other clouds,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.400
and containerize them
in one fluid motion--

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.960
a great way to migrate
and modernize in parallel.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.400
What about data?

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.680
If you've got a
treasure trove of data

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.690
and you don't want to
migrate it over a network,

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.000
you can load it up into
our transfer appliance

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.400
and ship it to us
directly so we can quickly

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.390
upload all that data for you.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.920
These are just three migration
options Google offers.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.590
So when it's time to decide,
knowing which approach

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.050
can save you the
most time and money

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.290
is a question our professional
services team and partners can

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.150
help you answer.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.730
Keep in mind that
migrating also doesn't

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:32.730
have to mean entirely abandoning
your on-prem servers either.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.590
In fact, by definition, you'll
be in a hybrid cloud state

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.990
while any migration
project progresses.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.660
But with Anthos, we
make managing hybrid

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.480
and multicloud easier
than ever before,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.570
giving you the flexibility
to craft your ideal hybrid

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.220
or multicloud, even
while performing

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.100
on migration in parallel.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.440
Learn about the most
common patterns of cloud

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.440
hybridization and
multicloud here.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.960
Now if after the
analysis, you determine

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.010
